      "content": "Writing off the chair as a one-time expense can be tempting, but it's essential to understand the risks. If you claim the entire cost as a business expense without proper documentation, you may be more likely to be audited.\n\nThe IRS uses a variety of methods to determine the business use percentage of a home office asset, including:\n\n1. The Simplified Option: This method allows you to deduct $5 per square foot of home office space, up to a maximum of $1,500. However, this method doesn't account for the business use percentage of specific assets like furniture.\n2. The Actual Expenses Method: You can deduct the actual expenses related to the chair, such as the cost of the chair itself, shipping, and installation. However, you'll need to keep detailed records of the business use percentage.\n3. The Business Use Percentage Method: You can estimate the business use percentage of the chair and deduct that amount. However, this method requires you to keep records of the chair's business use, such as photos, receipts, and logs of business hours spent using the chair.\n\nIf you're audited, the IRS may ask for documentation to support your business use percentage, such as:\n\n* Photos of the chair in your home",
